# ElectionGuard 2.1.0, section 3.3.7, Verification 6: one branch of
# the disjunctive Chaum-Pedersen range proof that a selection encrypts
# a value in [0, R].  Branch j divides the second target by K^j, which
# is public.  cryptosith.org/papers/EG-spec-v2.1.0.pdf
#
# Clean, and for a reason worth recording.  Check 6.A puts alpha and
# beta in the prime-order subgroup, and K is the election public key
# fixed by the key ceremony with its own Schnorr proofs, so neither
# base can be the identity and neither is chosen by the prover.
secrets xi
claims  xi
eq  alpha      g
eq  beta/K^j   K

# An instance: one distinct generator per base name, so the
# environment is faithful and the verdict above transfers to it.
# The exponents are ours and carry no meaning; every element of a
# prime-order group is a generator power, so this is simply a way
# to write distinct elements down.
point g  2
point K  3
